WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:03.390
Instructor: Next up, I'd like to talk about who uses Git.

00:03.390 --> 00:06.390
This will be pretty quick as well, and optional.

00:06.390 --> 00:09.630
You don't have to know about this in order to use it.

00:09.630 --> 00:13.050
But the first most obvious group would be engineers,

00:13.050 --> 00:14.730
developers, coders,

00:14.730 --> 00:16.470
ranging from people who work on

00:16.470 --> 00:21.270
their own little tiny side projects or just hobby projects,

00:21.270 --> 00:23.880
entrepreneurs up to massive tech giants

00:23.880 --> 00:25.650
like Facebook and Google.

00:25.650 --> 00:29.190
Git is the most commonly used version control system

00:29.190 --> 00:30.270
as we talked about.

00:30.270 --> 00:32.820
And when you're developing applications,

00:32.820 --> 00:35.640
version control is very, very important to use.

00:35.640 --> 00:37.470
On Git's official homepage,

00:37.470 --> 00:39.870
they list some of the companies that use it.

00:39.870 --> 00:43.560
Google, Facebook, Microsoft, Twitter, LinkedIn, Netflix.

00:43.560 --> 00:46.680
And then if you go to a website like StackShare,

00:46.680 --> 00:48.317
where you can view,

00:48.317 --> 00:50.520
I don't know how accurate StackShare is,

00:50.520 --> 00:52.560
but you can view different tools

00:52.560 --> 00:54.540
and companies that use them.

00:54.540 --> 00:56.970
There's other companies like Robinhood, Reddit,

00:56.970 --> 00:59.580
Lyft, StackShare itself uses Git.

00:59.580 --> 01:03.600
Smaller companies, Patreon, Fiverr, Canva,

01:03.600 --> 01:05.010
I use Canva all the time.

01:05.010 --> 01:06.750
Massive companies like Walmart.

01:06.750 --> 01:08.760
Anyway, the point is it's used by pretty much

01:08.760 --> 01:10.830
any engineering team you can think of.

01:10.830 --> 01:12.990
Also, another group that uses Git,

01:12.990 --> 01:15.420
or at least would benefit from being familiar

01:15.420 --> 01:17.910
with Git are tech adjacent roles.

01:17.910 --> 01:20.370
So people who are not developers

01:20.370 --> 01:23.040
but work with developers or deal with developers,

01:23.040 --> 01:26.220
learning the basics of Git is not all that difficult

01:26.220 --> 01:28.440
and it can help a lot with conversations,

01:28.440 --> 01:31.830
with deadlines, with just, you know, setting expectations.

01:31.830 --> 01:32.790
And in particular,

01:32.790 --> 01:35.940
designers often need the basic understanding

01:35.940 --> 01:39.120
to be able to work with Git and communicate with developers.

01:39.120 --> 01:41.760
So another completely separate group

01:41.760 --> 01:44.160
of people who are using Git these days

01:44.160 --> 01:46.830
are governments and large organizations.

01:46.830 --> 01:47.940
Not technical, right?

01:47.940 --> 01:50.217
They're not, I don't know, tech startups.

01:50.217 --> 01:54.720
Governments use Git a lot to manage the drafting of laws,

01:54.720 --> 01:57.840
collaboration of drafting laws,

01:57.840 --> 02:00.750
sharing data and large data sets.

02:00.750 --> 02:03.750
So Washington DC City Council uses Git and GitHub.

02:03.750 --> 02:05.250
We'll talk about GitHub in a bit,

02:05.250 --> 02:06.480
to publish its laws

02:06.480 --> 02:09.630
and have made it a collaborative process.

02:09.630 --> 02:10.860
Citizens have actually found

02:10.860 --> 02:14.250
and fixed typos collaboratively in those laws.

02:14.250 --> 02:15.990
They've fixed them using Git.

02:15.990 --> 02:18.330
So I think I have some examples.

02:18.330 --> 02:19.163
Here we are.

02:19.163 --> 02:21.150
This is the city of Chattanooga.

02:21.150 --> 02:23.310
They use GitHub, which again,

02:23.310 --> 02:26.760
we'll talk about what GitHub is and how it compares to Git,

02:26.760 --> 02:29.040
but all you need to know is that it's based on Git.

02:29.040 --> 02:30.660
This project is simple.

02:30.660 --> 02:34.470
It just shares parking locations in the city of Chattanooga.

02:34.470 --> 02:36.240
And then if we go to GitHub's webpage,

02:36.240 --> 02:39.488
they have something called governments.github.com.

02:39.488 --> 02:42.780
They show actually a whole bunch of different governments

02:42.780 --> 02:46.410
that use GitHub, which again is built on top of Git.

02:46.410 --> 02:49.080
So governments, groups from Argentina,

02:49.080 --> 02:51.600
Australia, the US, the UK, I mean,

02:51.600 --> 02:54.360
pretty much every country is listed here.

02:54.360 --> 02:55.860
Now, that doesn't mean that Git

02:55.860 --> 02:58.680
plays a central role in all of these governments.

02:58.680 --> 03:03.680
For example, let's see, USGS, the US Geological Survey.

03:05.040 --> 03:07.860
They have a whole bunch of things on GitHub.

03:07.860 --> 03:09.720
Pages and pages of these projects

03:09.720 --> 03:11.040
that are shared with the public

03:11.040 --> 03:13.530
and they use Git to manage all of them.

03:13.530 --> 03:17.460
Anyway, so that's another group that use Git these days.

03:17.460 --> 03:21.450
Scientists also more and more are using Git

03:21.450 --> 03:24.270
to help collaborate on projects,

03:24.270 --> 03:26.010
to share findings and data sets,

03:26.010 --> 03:29.640
to manage code and work together on code bases.

03:29.640 --> 03:34.290
Git and GitHub together really work well in a open source

03:34.290 --> 03:38.130
or a peer reviewed world like science and research.

03:38.130 --> 03:39.330
We'll learn more about GitHub.

03:39.330 --> 03:41.013
I keep saying that, but we will.

03:42.030 --> 03:42.930
Some other groups.

03:42.930 --> 03:44.250
Writers.

03:44.250 --> 03:45.510
It's not all writers, certainly.

03:45.510 --> 03:46.860
It's not that common these days,

03:46.860 --> 03:50.520
but some writers really are becoming fans of Git.

03:50.520 --> 03:52.380
I listen to some screenwriting podcasts

03:52.380 --> 03:53.940
to fall asleep to sometimes,

03:53.940 --> 03:56.340
and every so often there's an episode about using Git

03:56.340 --> 03:59.280
to write screenplays, manage versions, right?

03:59.280 --> 04:01.440
I talked about having different essay versions.

04:01.440 --> 04:03.720
Well, with a screenplay, you've got so many revisions,

04:03.720 --> 04:04.553
so many drafts.

04:04.553 --> 04:06.630
Or a novel, so many characters,

04:06.630 --> 04:09.240
so many ways of structuring a novel,

04:09.240 --> 04:10.980
ordering things, different arcs.

04:10.980 --> 04:14.100
So Git is definitely gaining in popularity there.

04:14.100 --> 04:15.330
And then specifically,

04:15.330 --> 04:17.670
I've seen multiple use cases of Git

04:17.670 --> 04:20.070
for collaborative textbook writing

04:20.070 --> 04:22.560
where textbooks usually have multiple authors.

04:22.560 --> 04:27.560
So this is a book, a textbook on informal homotopy.

04:27.720 --> 04:31.350
Homoto, I think it's homotopy type theory.

04:31.350 --> 04:34.290
I have no clue what it is, what the book,

04:34.290 --> 04:36.420
I mean, this means nothing to me other than the fact

04:36.420 --> 04:38.850
that it's a textbook on something crazy sounding.

04:38.850 --> 04:42.390
But a lot of people have used this textbook.

04:42.390 --> 04:46.756
It looks like a couple of thousand people have started.

04:46.756 --> 04:49.830
So it's like a favorite of their projects on GitHub.

04:49.830 --> 04:52.740
And here we'll see there's 85 people

04:52.740 --> 04:55.170
who have collaborated to write this textbook.

04:55.170 --> 04:56.945
Just one simple example.

04:56.945 --> 04:59.160
Now the last slide.

04:59.160 --> 05:01.200
Anyone really can use Git.

05:01.200 --> 05:03.090
People find all sorts of creative uses,

05:03.090 --> 05:04.800
from keeping a daily diary

05:04.800 --> 05:07.260
to writing their PhD thesis with Git,

05:07.260 --> 05:08.880
all the way to composers.

05:08.880 --> 05:11.640
I saw a Reddit comment from a music,

05:11.640 --> 05:13.530
a composer who writes symphonies,

05:13.530 --> 05:16.470
and he uses Git to help track the massive changes.

05:16.470 --> 05:18.600
All the different files, the parts,

05:18.600 --> 05:20.640
as his symphonies evolve over time.

05:20.640 --> 05:22.770
It's a month, multiple month process

05:22.770 --> 05:24.570
to write a symphony for a full orchestra

05:24.570 --> 05:26.670
with all the different instruments written out,

05:26.670 --> 05:28.830
he uses Git to help with that.

05:28.830 --> 05:31.200
So I assume a lot of you who are watching this

05:31.200 --> 05:35.760
are probably in this group right there, or maybe this group.

05:35.760 --> 05:38.490
But increasingly, I expect in the next few years

05:38.490 --> 05:40.410
there'll be more governments, more scientists,

05:40.410 --> 05:45.210
more writers, more symphony composers who use Git

05:45.210 --> 05:47.400
because it really can be,

05:47.400 --> 05:49.770
I won't say a life changer, maybe a life saver.

05:49.770 --> 05:52.380
It can save you a lot of stress and time down the line,

05:52.380 --> 05:54.540
assuming you can get past the initial hurdle

05:54.540 --> 05:55.530
of learning it.

05:55.530 --> 05:58.983
We are on the precipice of learning it, so I'll shut up.
